spec: specs/optics-outside.md (stage 6)

why: `Route.Of[C]` maps a route's tuple into a case class through
  `m.fromProduct`, which is POSITIONAL. The types are checked, so a
  real mismatch is caught — but the route's parameter NAMES and the
  class's field names are never compared, so
  `"id".as[Int] / "s".as[String]` fits `case class P(a: Int, b: String)`
  in silence. The operator asked for the check (2026-09-11).

what: `of[C]` becomes `inline`, reads `MirroredElemLabels` with
  `constValueTuple`, and refuses a route whose parameter names are not
  the class's field names, in order.

known and stated: the check is a RUN-TIME one at construction, and
  that is a property of the problem rather than a shortcut. The labels
  are type-level; the route's names are values in `Vector[Seg.Var]`.
  Lifting them into the type means a second type parameter
  (`Route[A, N <: Tuple]`) on every signature and every user
  annotation — the cost already declined once, when the path/query
  stages became two classes rather than one phantom parameter. Routes
  are declared as `val`s, so the check fires at class initialisation:
  start-up, before the first request, or never.

probe: scratchpad/probe/Labels.scala — labels read, mismatch refused
  with "route parameters (id, slug) do not match the field names (a, b)".

next: spec stage 6, the check, tests both ways, docs
